## The cycle

`crimes_00002` names it, 3 files, confidence 0.95:

```
src/application/billing-flow.ts  →  src/domain/billing.ts
src/domain/billing.ts            →  src/presentation/invoice-view.ts
src/presentation/invoice-view.ts →  src/application/billing-flow.ts
```

I read the fixture to confirm the edges and, more importantly, what's on them:

| File | Line | Edge |
|---|---|---|
| `src/application/billing-flow.ts:1` | `import { chargeCustomer } from "../domain/billing.js"` | app → domain |
| `src/domain/billing.ts:2` | `import { renderInvoice } from "../presentation/invoice-view.js"` | domain → presentation |
| `src/presentation/invoice-view.ts:3` | `import { runBillingFlow } from "../application/billing-flow.js"` | presentation → app |

**The suspected runtime error is almost certainly not a module-init/TDZ failure — it's unbounded recursion.** Every edge is an unconditional call in the function body: `runBillingFlow()` → `chargeCustomer(100)` → `renderInvoice(amount)` → `runBillingFlow()` → … Calling any of the three blows the stack (`RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`). crimes tells you the ring exists; it doesn't tell you the ring is also a live call cycle, so don't stop at "tree-shaking is unreliable" in the finding summary.

## Break `src/domain/billing.ts:2` — the domain → presentation import

Of the three edges, exactly one is independently wrong, and the scan says so itself: `crime_00003` flags that same import as a Layer Violation against `crimes.config.json`'s `domain cannotImport presentation`. The other two edges are legal under the configured rules (`application → domain` is allowed; `presentation → application` isn't in any `cannotImport` list). So one deletion kills the cycle *and* the layer violation, and you don't have to invent a policy call about which of two legal imports to sacrifice.

The design reason lines up with the config: `chargeCustomer` is domain logic that currently reaches downward for a string formatter. Make it return a value and let the caller render.

```ts
// src/domain/billing.ts — now a leaf, no imports
export interface Charge { amount: number }
export function chargeCustomer(amount: number): Charge {
  return { amount };
}

// src/presentation/invoice-view.ts — renders a value it is handed
import type { Charge } from "../domain/billing.js";
export function renderInvoice(charge: Charge): string {
  return `Invoice: ${charge.amount}`;
}

// src/application/billing-flow.ts — the only orchestrator
import { chargeCustomer } from "../domain/billing.js";
import { renderInvoice } from "../presentation/invoice-view.js";
export function runBillingFlow(): string {
  return renderInvoice(chargeCustomer(100));
}
```

That also drops `presentation → application`, which is what made the recursion self-sustaining. If you only want the minimum edit, cutting `domain/billing.ts:2` alone is enough to break the ring.

Two notes on the alternatives:

- **`import type` won't work here.** The suggested action offers it, but all three edges carry called values, not types. It's only usable on the new `Charge` import above.
- **Breaking `presentation/invoice-view.ts:3` instead** also opens the ring, but leaves `domain → presentation` in place — you'd still be sitting on `crime_00003`, and the domain layer would still be the thing that has to change when the view changes.

## The other two findings are unrelated to the cycle

`crime_00001` (Phantom Accomplice) and `crime_00004` (Deep Import Abuse) are the same line — `billing-flow.ts:4` importing `some-pkg/dist/internal/private/helper.js`, which no `package.json` declares. Worth fixing (a clean install can fail on it), but it isn't part of the ring and won't produce the recursion you're chasing.
